<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emirate of Córdoba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was the Muslim state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> founded in 756 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, a survivor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a> after its overthrow by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. It preserved Umayyad rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and was proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 929, continuing the legacy of the Umayyad Caliphate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Emirate of Córdoba" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DaniCBP · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caliphate_of_C%C3%B3rdoba_under_Almanzor_-_1000_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Umayyad conquest of the Visigothic Kingdom began in April 711, when an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>. Iberia became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, whose governors made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> their capital. In 750 the Umayyads lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Abd al-Rahman I, a prince of the deposed family, spent six years on the run, and in 756 he refused to recognise Abbasid authority and became independent emir of Córdoba. He united local fiefdoms in campaigns that reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and took over twenty-five years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> used the titles emir or sultan until the mid-10th century and nominally recognised the Abbasid caliphs, and their real control was always in question, especially along the Christian border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> came to the throne in 912. Facing the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, he took the title of caliph in 929. He secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, and brought the Christian kingdoms of the north under his influence by force.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, chamberlain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s title became symbolic, and the state came to rest on a professional army often drawn from outside the peninsula. After Almanzor died in 1002, rival factions competed for power. The civil war known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a> followed, and in 1031 the caliphate collapsed into independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under Umayyad rule Arabization and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> advanced significantly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> were a minority who formed the ruling elite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> a larger group, and indigenous converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, called Muwallads, became very numerous. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians held the status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, paying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was the cultural centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Its population probably passed 100,000 in the 10th century, and the royal library under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> held an estimated 400,000 to 500,000 volumes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s workshops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, bronzes and carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> boxes. The period left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> and Madinat al-Zahra, among the best-known works of Andalusi architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umayyad state of Córdoba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A fleet from the Umayyad <b>Emirate of Córdoba</b> under Asba' ibn Wakil joined the Aghlabids despite their rivalry; Ibn Kathir records 300 ships in all.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasids massacred most of the Umayyad family; the most famous survivor, Abd al-Rahman I, escaped to Spain and founded the <b>Emirate of Córdoba</b> in 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Abd al-Rahman had raised the <b>Emirate of Córdoba</b> to a caliphate in 929.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
